sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To describe a square on a given straight line.
----
proof:

Let *AB* be the given straight line.

It is required to describe a square on the straight line *AB.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="220" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.46 diagram" src="propI46.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.46",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;50,190",
            "B;point;free;170,190",
            "ABED;polygon;square;A,B;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "E;point;vertex;ABED,3",
            "D;point;vertex;ABED,4",
            "C;point;lineSlider;A,D,30,30",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Draw *AC* at right angles to the straight line *AB* from the point *A* on it. Make *AD* equal to *AB.* Draw *DE* through the point *D* parallel to *AB,* and draw *BE* through the point *B* parallel to *AD.* [!just I.11; I.3, I.31]

Then *ADEB* is a parallelogram. Therefore *AB* equals *DE,* and *AD* equals *BE.* [!just I.34]

But *AB* equals *AD,* therefore the four straight lines *BA, AD, DE,* and *EB* equal one another. Therefore the parallelogram *ADEB* is equilateral.

I say next that it is also right-angled.

Since the straight line *AD* falls upon the parallels *AB* and *DE,* therefore the sum of the angles *BAD* and *ADE* equals two right angles. [!just I.29]

But the angle *BAD* is right, therefore the angle *ADE* is also right. [!just I.Post.4]

And in parallelogrammic areas the opposite sides and angles equal one another, therefore each of the opposite angles *ABE* and *BED* is also right. Therefore *ADEB* is right-angled. [!just I.34]

And it was also proved equilateral.

Therefore it is a square, and it is described on the straight line *AB.* [!just I.Def.22]

<div class="qed">Q.E.F.</div>
----
guide:

We now have the second regular polygon, the first being the equilateral triangle of proposition @I.1. Regular polygons with 5, 6, and 15 sides are constructed in [Book IV](/elements/books/bookIV/).

#### Construction steps

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="280" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.46 diagram" src="propI46a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "I.46",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;100,160",
            "B;point;free;160,160",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "ABcirc;circle;radius;A,B",
            "F;point;extend;B,A,B,A",
            "AF;line;connect;A,F",
            "Fline;line;extend;A,F,B,A",
            "BFcirc;circle;radius;B,F",
            "FBcirc;circle;radius;F,B",
            "CC';line;bichord;BFcirc,FBcirc",
            "C;point;first;CC'",
            "D;point;cutoff;A,C,A,B",
            "BAcirc;circle;radius;B,A",
            "G;point;extend;A,B,A,B",
            "Bline;line;extend;A,B,F,B",
            "AGcirc;circle;radius;A,G",
            "GAcirc;circle;radius;G,A",
            "HH';line;bichord;GAcirc,AGcirc",
            "H;point;first;HH'",
            "E;point;cutoff;B,H,B,A",
            "ABED;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,E,D;0;0;black;random"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

There are quite a few steps needed to construct a square on *AB.* In order to construct the perpendicular *AC,* first *AB* has to be extended in the direction of *A* and a point *F* on the far side the same distance from *A* as *B* is, then two more circles centered at *B* and *F* to get a perpendicular line, and then it needs to be cut off at length *C,* but fortunately, the needed circle has already been drawn.

Next, *EB* is to be drawn through *B* parallel to *AD.* In general that construction given in @I.31 takes six circles, but in this case if *EB* is drawn perpendicular to *AB,* then it will be parallel to *AD,* too, and that construction only takes three circles with radii *BA, AG,* and *GA.*

This abbreviation of Euclid's construction requires six circles and four lines. There are alternate constructions that are a bit shorter.

#### Use of Proposition 46

The construction of a square given in this proposition is used in the next proposition, numerous propositions in Book II, and others in Books VI, XII, and XIII.
